#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:370
#, no-wrap
msgid "CHUNK LOOKUP:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:373
msgid "(C + 1) * 12 bytes providing the chunk offsets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:375
msgid "First 4 bytes describe chunk id. Value 0 is a terminating label."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:379
msgid ""
"Other 8 bytes provide offset in current file for chunk to start.  (Chunks "
"are provided in file-order, so you can infer the length using the next chunk "
"position if necessary.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:382
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:430
msgid ""
"The CHUNK LOOKUP matches the table of contents from the chunk-based file "
"format, see linkgit:gitformat-chunk[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:385
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:434
msgid ""
"The remaining data in the body is described one chunk at a time, and these "
"chunks may be given in any order. Chunks are required unless otherwise "
"specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:386
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:436
#, no-wrap
msgid "CHUNK DATA:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:388
#, no-wrap
msgid "Packfile Names (ID: {'P', 'N', 'A', 'M'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:393
msgid ""
"Stores the packfile names as concatenated, null-terminated strings.  "
"Packfiles must be listed in lexicographic order for fast lookups by "
"name. This is the only chunk not guaranteed to be a multiple of four bytes "
"in length, so should be the last chunk for alignment reasons."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:393
#, no-wrap
msgid "OID Fanout (ID: {'O', 'I', 'D', 'F'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:397
msgid ""
"The ith entry, F[i], stores the number of OIDs with first byte at most "
"i. Thus F[255] stores the total number of objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:397
#, no-wrap
msgid "OID Lookup (ID: {'O', 'I', 'D', 'L'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:400
msgid ""
"The OIDs for all objects in the MIDX are stored in lexicographic order in "
"this chunk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:400
#, no-wrap
msgid "Object Offsets (ID: {'O', 'O', 'F', 'F'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:402
msgid "Stores two 4-byte values for every object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:403
msgid "The pack-int-id for the pack storing this object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:412
msgid ""
"The offset within the pack.  If all offsets are less than `2^32`, then the "
"large offset chunk will not exist and offsets are stored as in IDX v1.  If "
"there is at least one offset value larger than `2^32-1`, then the large "
"offset chunk must exist, and offsets larger than `2^31-1` must be stored in "
"it instead. If the large offset chunk exists and the 31st bit is on, then "
"removing that bit reveals the row in the large offsets containing the 8-byte "
"offset of this object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:412
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Optional] Object Large Offsets (ID: {'L', 'O', 'F', 'F'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:414
msgid "8-byte offsets into large packfiles."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:414
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Optional] Bitmap pack order (ID: {'R', 'I', 'D', 'X'})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:418
msgid ""
"A list of MIDX positions (one per object in the MIDX, num_objects in total, "
"each a 4-byte unsigned integer in network byte order), sorted according to "
"their relative bitmap/pseudo-pack positions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:419
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "TRAILER:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:427
msgid ""
"(C + 1) * 12 bytes providing the chunk offsets: First 4 bytes describe chunk "
"id. Value 0 is a terminating label.  Other 8 bytes provide offset in current "
"file for chunk to start.  (Chunks are provided in file-order, so you can "
"infer the length using the next chunk position if necessary.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:443
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tPackfile Names (ID: {'P', 'N', 'A', 'M'})\n"
"\t    Store the names of packfiles as a sequence of NUL-terminated\n"
"\t    strings. There is no extra padding between the filenames,\n"
"\t    and they are listed in lexicographic order. The chunk itself\n"
"\t    is padded at the end with between 0 and 3 NUL bytes to make the\n"
"\t    chunk size a multiple of 4 bytes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tBitmapped Packfiles (ID: {'B', 'T', 'M', 'P'})\n"
"\t    Stores a table of two 4-byte unsigned integers in network order.\n"
"\t    Each table entry corresponds to a single pack (in the order that\n"
"\t    they appear above in the `PNAM` chunk). The values for each table\n"
"\t    entry are as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:451
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first bit position (in pseudo-pack order, see below) to\n"
"contain an object from that pack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:452
#, no-wrap
msgid "The number of bits whose objects are selected from that pack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:457
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"OID Fanout (ID: {'O', 'I', 'D', 'F'})\n"
"The ith entry, F[i], stores the number of OIDs with first\n"
"byte at most i. Thus F[255] stores the total\n"
"number of objects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:461
msgid ""
"OID Lookup (ID: {'O', 'I', 'D', 'L'})  The OIDs for all objects in the MIDX "
"are stored in lexicographic order in this chunk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:474
msgid ""
"Object Offsets (ID: {'O', 'O', 'F', 'F'})  Stores two 4-byte values for "
"every object.  1: The pack-int-id for the pack storing this object.  2: The "
"offset within the pack.  If all offsets are less than 2^32, then the large "
"offset chunk will not exist and offsets are stored as in IDX v1.  If there "
"is at least one offset value larger than 2^32-1, then the large offset chunk "
"must exist, and offsets larger than 2^31-1 must be stored in it instead. If "
"the large offset chunk exists and the 31st bit is on, then removing that bit "
"reveals the row in the large offsets containing the 8-byte offset of this "
"object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:477
msgid ""
"[Optional] Object Large Offsets (ID: {'L', 'O', 'F', 'F'})  8-byte offsets "
"into large packfiles."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:482
msgid ""
"[Optional] Bitmap pack order (ID: {'R', 'I', 'D', 'X'})  A list of MIDX "
"positions (one per object in the MIDX, num_objects in total, each a 4-byte "
"unsigned integer in network byte order), sorted according to their relative "
"bitmap/pseudo-pack positions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:486
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tIndex checksum of the above contents.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:487
#, no-wrap
msgid "multi-pack-index reverse indexes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:491
msgid ""
"Similar to the pack-based reverse index, the multi-pack index can also be "
"used to generate a reverse index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:497
msgid ""
"Instead of mapping between offset, pack-, and index position, this reverse "
"index maps between an object's position within the MIDX, and that object's "
"position within a pseudo-pack that the MIDX describes (i.e., the ith entry "
"of the multi-pack reverse index holds the MIDX position of ith object in "
"pseudo-pack order)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:503
msgid ""
"To clarify the difference between these orderings, consider a multi-pack "
"reachability bitmap (which does not yet exist, but is what we are building "
"towards here). Each bit needs to correspond to an object in the MIDX, and so "
"we need an efficient mapping from bit position to MIDX position."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:509
msgid ""
"One solution is to let bits occupy the same position in the oid-sorted index "
"stored by the MIDX. But because oids are effectively random, their resulting "
"reachability bitmaps would have no locality, and thus compress poorly. (This "
"is the reason that single-pack bitmaps use the pack ordering, and not the "
".idx ordering, for the same purpose.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:516
msgid ""
"So we'd like to define an ordering for the whole MIDX based around pack "
"ordering, which has far better locality (and thus compresses more "
"efficiently). We can think of a pseudo-pack created by the concatenation of "
"all of the packs in the MIDX. E.g., if we had a MIDX with three packs (a, b, "
"c), with 10, 15, and 20 objects respectively, we can imagine an ordering of "
"the objects like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:518
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:577
#, no-wrap
msgid "    |a,0|a,1|...|a,9|b,0|b,1|...|b,14|c,0|c,1|...|c,19|\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:522
msgid ""
"where the ordering of the packs is defined by the MIDX's pack list, and then "
"the ordering of objects within each pack is the same as the order in the "
"actual packfile."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:529
msgid ""
"Given the list of packs and their counts of objects, you can naïvely "
"reconstruct that pseudo-pack ordering (e.g., the object at position 27 must "
"be (c,1) because packs \"a\" and \"b\" consumed 25 of the slots). But "
"there's a catch. Objects may be duplicated between packs, in which case the "
"MIDX only stores one pointer to the object (and thus we'd want only one slot "
"in the bitmap)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:538
msgid ""
"Callers could handle duplicates themselves by reading objects in order of "
"their bit-position, but that's linear in the number of objects, and much too "
"expensive for ordinary bitmap lookups. Building a reverse index solves this, "
"since it is the logical inverse of the index, and that index has already "
"removed duplicates. But, building a reverse index on the fly can be "
"expensive. Since we already have an on-disk format for pack-based reverse "
"indexes, let's reuse it for the MIDX's pseudo-pack, too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:544
msgid ""
"Objects from the MIDX are ordered as follows to string together the "
"pseudo-pack. Let `pack(o)` return the pack from which `o` was selected by "
"the MIDX, and define an ordering of packs based on their numeric ID (as "
"stored by the MIDX). Let `offset(o)` return the object offset of `o` within "
"`pack(o)`. Then, compare `o1` and `o2` as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:547
msgid ""
"If one of `pack(o1)` and `pack(o2)` is preferred and the other is not, then "
"the preferred one sorts first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:551
msgid ""
"(This is a detail that allows the MIDX bitmap to determine which pack should "
"be used by the pack-reuse mechanism, since it can ask the MIDX for the pack "
"containing the object at bit position 0)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:554
msgid ""
"If `pack(o1) ≠ pack(o2)`, then sort the two objects in descending order "
"based on the pack ID."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:557
msgid ""
"Otherwise, `pack(o1) = pack(o2)`, and the objects are sorted in pack-order "
"(i.e., `o1` sorts ahead of `o2` exactly when `offset(o1)  < offset(o2)`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:561
msgid ""
"In short, a MIDX's pseudo-pack is the de-duplicated concatenation of objects "
"in packs stored by the MIDX, laid out in pack order, and the packs arranged "
"in MIDX order (with the preferred pack coming first)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:564
msgid ""
"The MIDX's reverse index is stored in the optional 'RIDX' chunk within the "
"MIDX itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:565
#, no-wrap
msgid "`BTMP` chunk"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:571
msgid ""
"The Bitmapped Packfiles (`BTMP`) chunk encodes additional information about "
"the objects in the multi-pack index's reachability bitmap. Recall that "
"objects from the MIDX are arranged in \"pseudo-pack\" order (see above) for "
"reachability bitmaps."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:575
msgid ""
"From the example above, suppose we have packs \"a\", \"b\", and \"c\", with "
"10, 15, and 20 objects, respectively. In pseudo-pack order, those would be "
"arranged as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:582
msgid ""
"When working with single-pack bitmaps (or, equivalently, multi-pack "
"reachability bitmaps with a preferred pack), linkgit:git-pack-objects[1] "
"performs ``verbatim'' reuse, attempting to reuse chunks of the bitmapped or "
"preferred packfile instead of adding objects to the packing list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:587
msgid ""
"When a chunk of bytes is reused from an existing pack, any objects contained "
"therein do not need to be added to the packing list, saving memory and CPU "
"time. But a chunk from an existing packfile can only be reused when the "
"following conditions are met:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:591
msgid ""
"The chunk contains only objects which were requested by the caller "
"(i.e. does not contain any objects which the caller didn't ask for "
"explicitly or implicitly)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:594
msgid ""
"All objects stored in non-thin packs as offset- or reference-deltas also "
"include their base object in the resulting pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:600
msgid ""
"The `BTMP` chunk encodes the necessary information in order to implement "
"multi-pack reuse over a set of packfiles as described above.  Specifically, "
"the `BTMP` chunk encodes three pieces of information (all 32-bit unsigned "
"integers in network byte-order) for each packfile `p` that is stored in the "
"MIDX, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:601
#, no-wrap
msgid "`bitmap_pos`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:603
msgid ""
"The first bit position (in pseudo-pack order) in the multi-pack index's "
"reachability bitmap occupied by an object from `p`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:604
#, no-wrap
msgid "`bitmap_nr`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:606
msgid ""
"The number of bit positions (including the one at `bitmap_pos`) that encode "
"objects from that pack `p`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:609
msgid ""
"For example, the `BTMP` chunk corresponding to the above example (with packs "
"``a'', ``b'', and ``c'') would look like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:625
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| |`bitmap_pos` |`bitmap_nr`\n"
"\n"
"|packfile ``a''\n"
"|`0`\n"
"|`10`\n"
"\n"
"|packfile ``b''\n"
"|`10`\n"
"|`15`\n"
"\n"
"|packfile ``c''\n"
"|`25`\n"
"|`20`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:631
msgid ""
"With this information in place, we can treat each packfile as individually "
"reusable in the same fashion as verbatim pack reuse is performed on "
"individual packs prior to the implementation of the `BTMP` chunk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:632
#, no-wrap
msgid "cruft packs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:638
msgid ""
"The cruft packs feature offer an alternative to Git's traditional mechanism "
"of removing unreachable objects. This document provides an overview of Git's "
"pruning mechanism, and how a cruft pack can be used instead to accomplish "
"the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:639
#, no-wrap
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:643
msgid ""
"To remove unreachable objects from your repository, Git offers `git repack "
"-Ad` (see linkgit:git-repack[1]). Quoting from the documentation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:648
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[...] unreachable objects in a previous pack become loose, unpacked "
"objects,\n"
"instead of being left in the old pack. [...] loose unreachable objects will "
"be\n"
"pruned according to normal expiry rules with the next 'git gc' invocation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:655
msgid ""
"Unreachable objects aren't removed immediately, since doing so could race "
"with an incoming push which may reference an object which is about to be "
"deleted.  Instead, those unreachable objects are stored as loose objects and "
"stay that way until they are older than the expiration window, at which "
"point they are removed by linkgit:git-prune[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:664
msgid ""
"Git must store these unreachable objects loose in order to keep track of "
"their per-object mtimes. If these unreachable objects were written into one "
"big pack, then either freshening that pack (because an object contained "
"within it was re-written) or creating a new pack of unreachable objects "
"would cause the pack's mtime to get updated, and the objects within it would "
"never leave the expiration window. Instead, objects are stored loose in "
"order to keep track of the individual object mtimes and avoid a situation "
"where all cruft objects are freshened at once."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:673
msgid ""
"This can lead to undesirable situations when a repository contains many "
"unreachable objects which have not yet left the grace period. Having large "
"directories in the shards of `.git/objects` can lead to decreased "
"performance in the repository. But given enough unreachable objects, this "
"can lead to inode starvation and degrade the performance of the whole "
"system. Since we can never pack those objects, these repositories often take "
"up a large amount of disk space, since we can only zlib compress them, but "
"not store them in delta chains."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:674
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cruft packs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:679
msgid ""
"A cruft pack eliminates the need for storing unreachable objects in a loose "
"state by including the per-object mtimes in a separate file alongside a "
"single pack containing all loose objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:687
msgid ""
"A cruft pack is written by `git repack --cruft` when generating a new pack.  "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]'s `--cruft` option. Note that `git repack "
"--cruft` is a classic all-into-one repack, meaning that everything in the "
"resulting pack is reachable, and everything else is unreachable. Once "
"written, the `--cruft` option instructs `git repack` to generate another "
"pack containing only objects not packed in the previous step (which equates "
"to packing all unreachable objects together). This progresses as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:691
msgid ""
"Enumerate every object, marking any object which is (a) not contained in a "
"kept-pack, and (b) whose mtime is within the grace period as a traversal "
"tip."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:694
msgid ""
"Perform a reachability traversal based on the tips gathered in the previous "
"step, adding every object along the way to the pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:697
msgid ""
"Write the pack out, along with a `.mtimes` file that records the per-object "
"timestamps."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:702
msgid ""
"This mode is invoked internally by linkgit:git-repack[1] when instructed to "
"write a cruft pack. Crucially, the set of in-core kept packs is exactly the "
"set of packs which will not be deleted by the repack; in other words, they "
"contain all of the repository's reachable objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:708
msgid ""
"When a repository already has a cruft pack, `git repack --cruft` typically "
"only adds objects to it. An exception to this is when `git repack` is given "
"the `--cruft-expiration` option, which allows the generated cruft pack to "
"omit expired objects instead of waiting for linkgit:git-gc[1] to expire "
"those objects later on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:711
msgid ""
"It is linkgit:git-gc[1] that is typically responsible for removing expired "
"unreachable objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:712
#, no-wrap
msgid "Alternatives"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:715
msgid "Notable alternatives to this design include:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:717
msgid "The location of the per-object mtime data."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-pack.txt:722
msgid ""
"On the location of mtime data, a new auxiliary file tied to the pack was "
"chosen to avoid complicating the `.idx` format. If the `.idx` format were "
"ever to gain support for optional chunks of data, it may make sense to "
"consolidate the `.mtimes` format into the `.idx` itself."
msgstr ""

